Secure Boot enables kernel integrity lockdown on Ubuntu, and lockdown
rejects hibernation because an unauthenticated resume image can replace
kernel memory. Add a default-off Kconfig policy switch for systems whose
threat model permits traditional hibernation from encrypted swap.

This does not authenticate the resume image. It must not be enabled on a
kernel expected to defend against a privileged attacker who can modify the
image while its encrypted storage is open.

diff --git a/kernel/power/Kconfig b/kernel/power/Kconfig
--- a/kernel/power/Kconfig
+++ b/kernel/power/Kconfig
@@ -92,6 +92,18 @@
 
 	  If in doubt, say Y.
 
+config HIBERNATION_ALLOW_LOCKDOWN
+	bool "Allow hibernation while kernel lockdown is active"
+	depends on HIBERNATION
+	default n
+	help
+	  Permit suspend-to-disk and resume while the kernel lockdown LSM is
+	  active. A resume image can replace kernel memory, so enabling this
+	  option weakens lockdown integrity unless the image is authenticated.
+
+	  Enable this only when the swap storage and privileged users are
+	  trusted. If in doubt, say N.
+
 choice
 	prompt "Default compressor"
 	default HIBERNATION_COMP_LZO
diff --git a/kernel/power/hibernate.c b/kernel/power/hibernate.c
--- a/kernel/power/hibernate.c
+++ b/kernel/power/hibernate.c
@@ -109,7 +109,8 @@
 bool hibernation_available(void)
 {
 	return nohibernate == 0 &&
-		!security_locked_down(LOCKDOWN_HIBERNATION) &&
+		(IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HIBERNATION_ALLOW_LOCKDOWN) ||
+		 !security_locked_down(LOCKDOWN_HIBERNATION)) &&
 		!secretmem_active() && !cxl_mem_active();
 }
 
